WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.389
We started off by talking about energy and that complete one-for-one

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:06.899
relationship between the size of the economy and the amount of energy

00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:11.460
generated. So let's check back in. Look
what's happened happening since 1970.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:16.020
Here's where we were with energy use up until the first Earth Day. Here's what's

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:22.050
been happening in the years since. We are decoupling—profoundly—the growth of our

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.590
economy, the growth of our society, and our prosperity, from the total amount of

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:31.560
joules, or BTUs, that you need to generate that economy. Our economy is about 25

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.090
percent bigger than it was at the end of
the Great Recession. Our energy use is

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.920
essentially flat all throughout that
time. The added good news here is that

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.190
largely because of the fracking revolution, where we're burning less coal

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.460
and using more natural gas to generate electricity,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.949
our total greenhouse gas emissions are
actually now trending downward over time.

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:57.480
And that red line is really interesting;
it comes from a data source that takes

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.280
into account the carbon generated by all
the products that we import. So this

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:07.140
really is the accurate picture of the
carbon footprint of the American economy.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.720
And I think it is past its peak, it's
trending down, and I will take anyone's

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.590
bet that we're not going to hit that high point ever again—or at least for

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:21.540
the next ten years. Now, I want to say
this really clearly: this line is not

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:26.450
going down quickly enough. I am NOT
encouraging any kind of complacency here.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:32.400
But we have finally decoupled growing
our economy from generating greenhouse

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.100
gases in a pretty deep way.

